  nothing you've asked for.

  [1mB. Named-rule config object[0m
  Same schema position, but rules are data: [38;5;153m{ username: [{ rule: "required" }, { rule: "minLength", value: 3 }] }[39m,
  resolved against a registry of built-in rule names inside the module.
  [3mBuys:[0m the schema is plain JSON — serializable, server-shareable, storable in a config file. [3mCosts:[0m every custom rule
  means editing the registry inside the shared module, so form-specific logic leaks into shared code. More machinery for
  the same result. [3mWhen it wins:[0m if validation rules ever need to come from a server or a config file.

  [1mC. Markup-driven via data attributes[0m
  Rules live in the HTML: [38;5;153m<input name="email" data-validate="required|email" data-label="Email">[39m. The module scans the
  form on attach; forms need zero JS config.
  [3mBuys:[0m the lightest per-form footprint — add a form, add attributes, done. Rules sit next to the fields they govern.
  [3mCosts:[0m you're inventing a string mini-language, and parameterized rules get ugly fast ([38;5;153mminLength:8[39m needs parsing and
  has no type checking). Validation logic becomes invisible to JS tooling and awkward to test. [3mWhen it wins:[0m many simple
  forms authored by people who don't touch the JS.

  [1mRecommendation: A.[0m B's one real advantage is serialization, and your answers rule out the cases that need it (no
  async/server rules, no config-driven forms) — so it's paying machinery cost for a benefit you won't collect. C's cost
  lands exactly where this repo is weakest: it has no tests, and C is the hardest of the three to test. A gives the same
  declarative reading as B at the call site while keeping every rule a plain function.

  Two details A pulls in, worth flagging now:
  - [1mFields are looked up by [38;5;153mname[39m, not [38;5;153mid[39m.[0m [38;5;153mindex.html[39m's inputs have [38;5;153mid[39m but no [38;5;153mname[39m, so this adds [38;5;153mname[39m attributes. That's
    the standard form idiom and lets the module read values generically instead of a hardcoded [38;5;153mgetElementById[39m per field.
  - [1mThe error markup convention becomes a contract.[0m My proposal: for each invalid field the module finds-or-creates
    [38;5;153m<span class="field-error" data-error-for="<name>">[39m right after the input, and sets [38;5;153maria-invalid[39m plus
    [38;5;153maria-describedby[39m on the input so screen readers announce the error. Every form inherits this, so changing it later
    means touching all of them.

  Does approach A look right, and does that error-markup convention work for you? Once you confirm, I'll walk the design
  section by section, then write the spec to [38;5;153mdocs/hyperpowers/specs/2026-09-16-reusable-form-validation-design.md[39m for
  your review before any code gets written.

